  "corrected_content": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-125460",
    "knowledge_point_id": "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating Circulation",
    "problem": "Calculate the circulation $\u0393$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i-\\frac{5}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Interpret circulation as the sum of the four convergent improper line integrals over the open quadrant arcs, taking independent one-sided limits at $(0,\\pm1)$ and $(\\pm1,0)$. Equivalently, integrate the continuous extension of the scalar pullback $F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)$. The field itself remains undefined at the four axis intersections, so the original ordinary circulation is undefined.",
    "worked_solution": "The field is undefined where $x=0$ or $y=0$, and the unit circle passes through four such points. Thus the original ordinary circulation is undefined. Under the stated improper-integral convention, the given parameterization is clockwise and\n\n$$r(t)=(\\sin t,\\cos t),\\qquad r\\prime(t)=(\\cos t,-\\sin t).$$\n\nOn each open quadrant arc, both coordinates are nonzero, so\n\n$$F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)=\\frac{5}{\\cos t}\\cos t+\\left(-\\frac{5}{\\sin t}\\right)(-\\sin t)=10.$$\n\nEvery one-sided endpoint limit of this scalar pullback is $10$. Each arc integral converges absolutely, independently of the other arcs. With $a_k=k\\pi/2$,\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=\\sum_{k=0}^{3}\\lim_{\\epsilon_k\\downarrow0,\\,\\delta_k\\downarrow0}\\int_{a_k+\\epsilon_k}^{a_{k+1}-\\delta_k}10\\,dt=4(5\\pi)=20\\pi.$$\n\nThis also equals the integral of the scalar pullback extended continuously by $10$ at its missing points. Only the scalar pullback is extended; the vector field is not assigned finite limits there. No principal-value cancellation or application of Green's theorem is needed.",
