    "q-125457": {
      "problem": "Calculate the circulation $\u0393$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$",
      "authentic_grade": "Correct",
      "worked_solution": "The circulation $\u0393$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\u0393={\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{\u2032}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)+(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0393={\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "source_files": {
