    "q-125585": {
      "problem": "Calculate the circulation $\u0393$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise.",
      "authentic_grade": "Correct",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\u0393$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\u0393={\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{\u2032}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0393={\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
